# Results: logistic regression vs k-NN on Iris (OpenML 61)

Generated by the code in `report/code/` (run from that directory):

```
python fetch_data.py                                   # OpenML 61 -> data/ (md5 checked)
python cv_study.py ../results/local                    # local run   (~30 s, 1 thread)
sbatch ibex_job.sbatch                                 # Ibex run    (writes results/ibex; copied here)
python compare_runs.py ../results/local ../results/ibex ../results/comparison.json
python make_outputs.py                                 # folds_long.csv, intervals.csv, reproducibility.json, numbers.tex
python sensitivity.py ../results/sensitivity.json      # robustness checks (not the primary result)
```
Packages: `report/code/requirements.txt` (identical versions on both machines; full `pip_freeze.txt` in each run directory).

## Design
- Data: `iris.arff` from https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff (OpenML 61, version 1), md5 `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9` = OpenML's published checksum; 150 rows, 4 numeric features, 3 classes x 50.
- Outer: `RepeatedStratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, n_repeats=10, random_state=20261017)`; 50 splits (120 train / 30 test) shared by both methods, so differences are paired.
- LR: StandardScaler + `LogisticRegression(C=1, lbfgs, multinomial softmax, max_iter=10000)`; fixed hyperparameters (only k is tuned, per the objective).
- k-NN: StandardScaler + `KNeighborsClassifier` (uniform weights, Euclidean); k in 1..30 chosen on each outer training fold by inner stratified 5-fold CV accuracy (`random_state=20261017+1000+i`), ties to the smallest k.
- Scaling is fitted inside each training fold. All BLAS/OpenMP threads = 1.
- 95% intervals over the 50 per-fold values: **primary = Nadeau–Bengio corrected repeated-CV t** (mean ± t(0.975, 49)·sqrt((1/50 + 30/120)·s²)). Naive t and percentile bootstrap over folds (10 000 resamples, seed 20261017) are reported only as a check: both treat the overlapping folds as independent and are too narrow.

## Results (`local/run.json`; Ibex identical)
| quantity | mean | corrected 95% CI (primary) | naive t | bootstrap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| LR accuracy | 95.40% | [91.56, 99.24] | [94.35, 96.45] | [94.33, 96.40] |
| k-NN accuracy | 94.47% | [89.46, 99.47] | [93.11, 95.83] | [93.13, 95.73] |
| LR − k-NN (pp) | +0.93 | [−2.51, 4.38] | [−0.01, 1.87] | [0.07, 1.87] |

- Corrected resampled t-test of a zero difference: p = 0.59. Per fold: LR better 16, tied 25, k-NN better 9 of 50.
- Exact McNemar per repetition on out-of-fold predictions: no repetition below p = 0.45 (`local/run.json` → `mcnemar_per_repeat`).
- Mean errors per 150 predictions: LR 6.9, k-NN 8.3. Chosen k ranges 1–21, median 8, mode 9 (7/50); k varies strongly between folds (`inner_cv.csv` has the inner accuracy for every k).
- **Reading:** the two methods are not distinguishable on Iris with this protocol. The corrected interval for the difference spans roughly −2.5 to +4.4 points; the naive intervals would suggest a small LR advantage only because they ignore the dependence between folds.

